BT /GS0 gs /T1_0 1 Tf 8 0 0 8 363.0025 58.8319 Tm (143)Tj ET q 1 0 0 1 100.345993 113.595993 cm 0.5 w  /GS1 gs 0 0 m 276 0 l S Q BT /GS1 gs /T1_1 1 Tf 7.75 0 0 7.75 100.3465 99.3468 Tm (Statistics at Square One)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf [(, XIth edition. By M.J. Campbell and T)74(.D.V)129(. Swinscow)74(. )]TJ 0 -1.355 TD (Published 2009 by Blackwell Publishing, ISBN: 9781405191005.)Tj ET 1 g /GS2 gs 100.346 504.846 276 71.283 re f BT 0 g /GS1 gs /T1_3 1 Tf 0.122 Tc 13 0 0 13 100.3465 566.8294 Tm (CHAPTER 13)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf 0 Tc 22 0 0 22 100.3465 541.0294 Tm (Study design and choosing )Tj 0 -1.091 TD (a statistical test)Tj /T1_3 1 Tf 11 0 0 11 100.3465 445.9139 Tm (Design)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf 0.147 Tw 9.25 0 0 9.25 100.3465 427.9139 Tm (In many ways the design of a study is more important than the )Tj 0.1152 Tw 0 -1.297 TD (analysis. A badly designed study can never be retrieved, whereas )Tj 0.192 Tw T* (a poorly analyzed one can usually be reanalyzed. Consideration )Tj 0.0647 Tw T* (of design is also important because the design of a study will gov-)Tj 0.0525 Tw T* (ern how the data are to be analyzed. This chapter can only briefly )Tj 0.1446 Tw T* (consider the issues and for more details the reader is referred to )Tj 0 Tw T* (Machin and Campbell.)Tj 6.475 0 0 6.475 193.7055 359.3369 Tm (1)Tj 0.017 Tw 9.25 0 0 9.25 109.5965 343.9139 Tm (Most medical studies consider an input, which may be a medical )Tj 0.0718 Tw -1 -1.297 Td (intervention or exposure to a potentially toxic compound, and an )Tj 0.1115 Tw T* (output, which is some measure of health that the intervention is )Tj 0.1148 Tw T* (supposed to affect. The simplest way to categorize studies is with )Tj 0.0593 Tw T* (reference to the time sequence in which the input and output are )Tj 0 Tw T* (studied.)Tj 0.1089 Tw 1 -1.297 Td (The most powerful studies are )Tj /T1_1 1 Tf 0 Tw (prospective)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf 0.1089 Tw ( studies, and the para-)Tj 0.2308 Tw -1 -1.297 Td (digm for these is the )Tj /T1_1 1 Tf 0.2075 Tw (randomized controlled trial)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf 0.2308 Tw (. In this, subjects )Tj 0.2576 Tw T* (with a disease are randomized to one of two \(or more\) treat-)Tj 0.0492 Tw T* (ments, one of which may be a control treatment. Methods of ran-)Tj 0.1132 Tw T* (domization have been described in Chapter 4. The importance of )Tj 0.1158 Tw T* (randomization is that we know in the long run \(i.e. with a large )Tj 0.0826 Tw T* (number of subjects\), treatment groups will be balanced in known )Tj 0 Tw T* (and )Tj /T1_1 1 Tf 2.051 0 Td (unknown)Tj /T1_2 1 Tf 0.1064 Tw [( prognostic factors. However)55(, with small studies, the )]TJ 0.2644 Tw -2.051 -1.297 Td (chances of imbalance in some important prognostic factors are )Tj -0.0018 Tw T* (quite high. Another important feature of randomization is that one )Tj 0.0951 Tw T* (cannot predict in advance which treatment a patient will receive. )Tj 0.225 Tw T* (This matters, because knowledge of likely treatment may affect )Tj ET 
